{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And Avimelekh king of Gerar sent and took Sarah.</b> After Sarah had been taken in Egypt by Pharaoh, and a great miracle had been done for him so that he was forced to return her against his will by means of great plagues — how could he now return to the same course in Gerar and say of her, 'She is my sister'? Was he relying on a miracle every single time? And perhaps no miracle would be done for him." "<b>And our Rabbi Hananel, of blessed memory, wrote</b> that now, when he came to Gerar, he divorced her, because he feared lest they kill him if he said she was his wife. And even so, HaShem, may He be exalted, did not let him be separated from her, and did not leave the righteous woman with the wicked man, for 'the rod of the wicked shall not rest upon the lot of the righteous' (Psalms 125:3). And this divorce was because of fear. And this is what the Holy One, blessed be He, said: והיא בעולת בעל — 'she had been covered by a husband' (Genesis 20:3) — meaning he did not divorce her except out of fear, for he was coerced. Thus the divorce is not a complete divorce. And this is what Avimelekh said: הגוי גם צדיק תהרוג — 'the nation, even a righteous one, would you kill?' (Genesis 20:4). That is to say, the nation that does not have this law, that even a coerced divorce is a divorce — and furthermore, he is righteous in that he did not approach her, [yet] you would kill him. And not only that, but he said to me, 'She is my sister,' and she too said, time after time, 'He is my brother' — 'with integrity of my heart and innocence of my hands I took her' (Genesis 20:5), inasmuch as I have not yet approached her."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:13:53.168837555Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}